500
01:35:00,551 --> 01:35:05,131
Are university campuses turning into mini smart cities?

501
01:35:05,332 --> 01:35:06,912
Think of a university campus.

502
01:35:07,231 --> 01:35:13,391
It has its own roads, shops, residential areas, banks and transport links.

503
01:35:13,391 --> 01:35:18,771
It may be visited by tens of thousands of people each day.

504
01:35:18,771 --> 01:35:22,351
It is in fact a tiny city.

505
01:35:23,051 --> 01:35:30,311
Across the globe, these mini metropolises are increasingly opting for a smart city approach.

506
01:35:30,931 --> 01:35:36,072
This is a tech-driven model that is used in places such as Barcelona,

507
01:35:36,492 --> 01:35:42,912
where street lamps react intelligently to surroundings to save energy.

508
01:35:42,912 --> 01:35:49,492
Seattle, where smart traffic lights respond to the conditions on the road.

509
01:35:49,832 --> 01:35:59,292
And even Milton Keynes, which has a real-time data hub sharing information about the town's energy and water consumption,

510
01:36:00,072 --> 01:36:03,612
transport, weather and pollution.

511
01:36:03,612 --> 01:36:08,452
Accordingly, universities are taking notice.

512
01:36:08,452 --> 01:36:16,471
The U.S. universities are leading the way with on-campus innovations around energy.

513
01:36:16,652 --> 01:36:23,471
The University of Texas at Austin has a fully independent grid that provides all its energy.

514
01:36:24,131 --> 01:36:24,832
Transport.

515
01:36:24,832 --> 01:36:29,692
The University of Michigan has introduced a self-driving shuttle system.

516
01:36:29,692 --> 01:36:30,971
And information.

517
01:36:31,192 --> 01:36:42,752
The University of Minnesota has installed 300 digital signage boards updated with real-time data.

518
01:36:42,752 --> 01:36:48,011
UK institutions are following them.

519
01:36:48,011 --> 01:36:54,851
The University of Glasgow has been working with the Innovation Center Future Cities Catapult

520
01:36:54,851 --> 01:37:02,731
on a strategy to bring smart tech to the campus as it expands.

521
01:37:02,731 --> 01:37:14,311
The vision includes intelligent campus AI, on-demand bus service, and a data center powered by renewable energy.

522
01:37:14,891 --> 01:37:22,271
It is not yet confirmed if all these will be implemented, but according to Cem Ejinti,

523
01:37:22,271 --> 01:37:32,332
one of the designers who worked on the strategy, universities are uniquely well-placed to experiment.

524
01:37:33,332 --> 01:37:37,692
Smart cities are kind of slow-moving, she says.

525
01:37:38,431 --> 01:37:45,051
Cities are so big, and there are so many players and stakeholders, it can be difficult.

526
01:37:45,492 --> 01:37:48,692
But universities have control over their estates.

527
01:37:49,311 --> 01:37:51,032
They own all the buildings.

528
01:37:51,032 --> 01:37:52,872
They own all the networks.

529
01:37:52,872 --> 01:37:57,912
And they have a captive audience in terms of the students.

530
01:37:58,351 --> 01:38:02,032
So they can become like a living lab.

531
01:38:02,672 --> 01:38:08,431
Many UK universities are doing fascinating things with tech, she says,

532
01:38:08,832 --> 01:38:18,712
but are often operating in a siloed way, department by department, rather than in a unison.

533
01:38:18,712 --> 01:38:27,811
It is something that Manchester Metropolitan University is attempting to address with its own smart campus plan,

534
01:38:28,152 --> 01:38:37,511
which unites six projects, including engagement monitoring, digital wayfinding, lecture capture, and cloud access.

535
01:38:37,511 --> 01:38:38,511
And that's what we're doing.

536
01:38:38,511 --> 01:38:53,891
So we're going to be doing this.

537
01:38:39,604 --> 01:38:49,724
The Smart Campus idea was first floated in spring 2016, says Tori Brown, the university's IT portfolio manager.

538
01:38:49,724 --> 01:39:06,164
As more projects and initiatives kept coming to light, it felt right to bring these together to tell a story around student engagement and how we can use technology to support this.

539
01:39:06,164 --> 01:39:10,024
It's a continually evolving plan, she continues.

540
01:39:10,824 --> 01:39:22,064
There are possibilities around smart kiosks with personalized information through cross-campus digital and personalized wayfinding.

541
01:39:22,404 --> 01:39:27,484
These include wearable texts like smartwatches and phones.

542
01:39:27,644 --> 01:39:35,804
For example, you have a lecture in 10 minutes in room X and building Y, then here is a map and directions.

543
01:39:36,164 --> 01:39:42,304
Have you remembered your assignment due in this class?

544
01:39:42,304 --> 01:39:52,384
As you've got time, if you leave now, you can also take back the library book that is due for return tomorrow.

545
01:39:52,384 --> 01:40:02,104
Duncan University in Victoria, Australia, has built and implemented a similar system named Gini.

546
01:40:02,104 --> 01:40:18,024
It's a digital assistant in the form of a Siri-style voice-activated smartphone app with information on assignments, timetables, referencing, and more.

547
01:40:18,024 --> 01:40:23,984
Because it runs on AI, it grows more useful as it is used.

548
01:40:23,984 --> 01:40:35,923
The university has a large distance learning cohort with 25% fully online and the other 75% acting like they are.

549
01:40:35,923 --> 01:40:54,844
According to Beverly Oliver, deputy vice-director for education at Deakin, as well as getting smarter, the system is producing a huge amount of that most prized modern commodity data.

550
01:40:54,844 --> 01:41:09,064
Oliver says the university has a strong policy around not watching students but using their data with their knowledge in order to help them.

551
01:41:09,064 --> 01:41:28,224
That is also the driving force at the university of Nottingham, according to the estates manager, Andy Nolan, where they are using data to understand how the physical space of the campus is used and adjust planning accordingly.

552
01:41:28,224 --> 01:41:31,284
It is still early days, he says.

553
01:41:31,864 --> 01:41:53,524
We want to do more research around human behavior, in particular so we can start to use things like the Wi-Fi network to monitor the presence of people as a proxy for football in different areas of the campus.

554
01:41:53,524 --> 01:42:07,564
We have done some early piloting of data capture, which was interesting, so we were looking at how we can roll it out more widely.

555
01:42:08,212 --> 01:42:13,352
But any kind of monitoring does raise questions about privacy.

556
01:42:13,952 --> 01:42:37,172
Curtin University in Western Australia has joined up with Hitachi to turn the campus into a data gathering laboratory with 1,600 cameras linked to facial recognition and analytics software to gather information on building trends, study patterns,

557
01:42:37,172 --> 01:42:40,552
and course attendance.

558
01:42:41,332 --> 01:42:54,752
According to the university's chief operating officer, Jan Callahan, this will be used to improve the student experience and enhance learning.

559
01:42:54,752 --> 01:43:07,332
Universities need to remember that data isn't a magic bullet, says Kathleen Armour, pro-wise rector for education at the University of Birmingham.

560
01:43:07,332 --> 01:43:18,012
I'm not convinced by the suggestion that we need to collect mountains of data on everything a student does, she says.

561
01:43:18,292 --> 01:43:22,012
It is easy to be carried away.

562
01:43:22,672 --> 01:43:34,852
Instead, we need to use anonymous data intelligently to ensure our campus support and systems are made as effective as possible to meet students' needs.

563
01:43:34,852 --> 01:43:52,852
Sean Waring, chief operating officer and deputy vice rector for education at London South Bank University, says it is the ownership and application of such data that should be a vital concern.

564
01:43:52,852 --> 01:44:00,412
The data should belong to the students more than the organizations, she says.

565
01:44:00,412 --> 01:44:16,212
It should be made available and students should be able to see what their attendance looks like compared to their peers and how their attendance may relate to their overall achievement.

566
01:44:16,212 --> 01:44:28,652
But we should be really wary of universities, owning data, owning that data and making judgments and adjusting their provision in relation to that data.

567
01:44:29,112 --> 01:44:36,452
It's part of the kind of civilian society we don't want to sleepwalk into.

568
01:44:37,132 --> 01:44:44,512
It's paternalistic and not a true partnership, not enabling the students as adults.

569
01:44:44,512 --> 01:44:54,872
It is not that the data isn't useful, but we don't know enough yet about how to use it carefully.

